=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** iptables FORWARD chain on router6 — three blanket DROP rules block all forwarded IP traffic except one narrow exception.

**Mechanism:** Router6 is the sole L3 gateway between the 172.18.51.0/24 site (mswitch2 side, pc15–18) and the rest of the network. Its iptables FORWARD chain contains: ACCEPT TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, then DROP TCP (all), DROP UDP (all), DROP proto 0 (all protocols). Protocol 0 in iptables matches any protocol — so ICMP, TCP, and UDP traffic that is forwarded through router6 all hit the DROP rules. Locally-sourced traffic from router6 itself bypasses the FORWARD chain (it hits OUTPUT), which is why router6 can ping pc16 directly while router3 cannot transit through it.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain. The DROP rules affect traffic entering on eth2 (10.50.1.0/24, r6_r3 link) destined for eth0/eth1 (172.18.50.x / 172.18.51.x), and vice versa. The underlying RIP routing tables and IP forwarding sysctl are both healthy — the kernel has the correct routes and ip_forward=1. The fault is entirely in netfilter.

**Root cause:** iptables FORWARD rules on router6 drop all transit traffic (TCP, UDP, all protocols) with only one exception — TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1 — rendering the 172.18.51.x subnet unreachable from every external subnet.

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain (or replace with correctly scoped rules), restoring normal transit forwarding through router6.
=== END ===